## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming skills for selecting exactly k disjoint subarrays with a per-subarray penalty—a common variant in coding interviews that extends standard maximum subarray problems.

## Key Concepts

- **Non-overlapping constraint**: Subarrays must not intersect, handled by the state machine.
- **Dual-state DP**: Maintaining both global (dp) and local (end) states to track ongoing vs. completed selections.
- **Reconstruction**: Tracing back decisions to recover actual subarray endpoints.
- **Optimization**: Balancing time complexity (O(nk)) against space (full vs. rolling arrays).

## Code Example

```javascript
const solve = (A, k, c) => {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const end = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Not taking a subarray ending at i
      if (dp[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'skip';
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = end[i][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'end';
      }

      // Taking/starting subarray at i
      if (t > 0) {
        const startNew = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c;
        const extend = end[i-1][t] + A[i-1] - c;
        end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction logic would go here
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: [] };
};
```

## Complexity

- **Time:** O(nk)
- **Space:** O(nk) for full DP, O(k) for optimized version

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: partitioning a string into k segments with a per-segment distinct-character score, a practical proxy for text analytics. It tests state formulation, precomputation, and reconstruction, with a baseline O(n^2k) DP suitable for beginner to intermediate levels.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitioning problems
- Precomputing substring properties in O(n^2)
- Reconstructing cuts via parent pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScorePartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    const seen = new Set();
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      seen.add(s[j]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction could be added here
  return {score: dp[n][k], cutStartIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) and compute dist on the fly?
- How does the approach change if k can be 0 or n+? and if n is large?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: interval-based value depends on distinct characters within each substring, plus precise reconstruction for k blocks.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping interval DP
- Precomputing distinct counts for all substrings
- Reconstruction via back-pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
function maxKSubstrings(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let setBits = 0;
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(j) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) setBits++;
      dist[j][i] = setBits;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  const start = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Option 1: skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prev[i][t] = i-1;
        start[i][t] = start[i-1][t];
      }

      // Option 2: end t-th substring at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s-1][t-1] + dist[s-1][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = s-1;
          start[i][t] = s-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct substrings
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t] === i-1) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.unshift([start[i][t]+1, i]);
      i = prev[i][t];
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], substrings: result };
}
```

Time complexity: O(n²k) for DP transitions + O(n²) for preprocessing. Space: O(n² + nk). Optimizations include limiting the inner loop or using convex hull tricks for special cases.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced tree DP with a connectivity constraint and a knapsack-like merge across children. Requires careful reconstruction of the chosen nodes.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP: dp[u][s] for connected subgraphs containing u within its subtree
- Knapsack-like merging of children to combine subtree sizes
- Path reconstruction: storing decisions to recover node set
- Complexity: O(nk) time and O(nk) space; handle negative values with offset

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxConnectedSubtreeSum(n, k, edges, val) {
  const g = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { g[u].push(v); g[v].push(u); }
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  const order = [];
  function dfs(u, p) {
    parent[u] = p;
    for (const v of g[u]) if (v !== p) dfs(v, u);
    order.push(u);
  }
  dfs(0, -1);
  // dp[u] will be an array of length min(subtreeSize[u], k) + 1
  // Initialization, merging and reconstruction would follow a standard tree knapsack pattern
  // Full implementation is lengthy; outline focuses on the recurrence above
  return { best: null, nodes: [] };
}
```

