## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests partition DP with a subarray range cost, requiring precomputation of subarray extrema and careful reconstruction. It adds a different objective than prior questions by using (max-min) rather than a function of endpoints or a fixed multiplier. 

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact-k segments
- Subarray range queries (max-min) via precomputation
- Path reconstruction from parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs for small to mid-size n

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  const minv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    maxv[i][i] = A[i];
    minv[i][i] = A[i];
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      maxv[i][j] = Math.max(maxv[i][j-1], A[j]);
      minv[i][j] = Math.min(minv[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = (maxv[0][i-1]-minv[0][i-1]) * i, par[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const cost = (maxv[p][i-1]-minv[p][i-1]) * (i-p);
        if (dp[p][t-1] + cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + cost;
          par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let idx = n, rem = k, cuts = [];
  while (rem > 0) {
    const p = par[idx][rem];
    cuts.push([p, idx-1]);
    idx = p; rem--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][k], blocks: cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP formulation for selecting fixed-count non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost, plus reconstruction of blocks.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i (prefix) and t (blocks)
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running best to avoid O(n^2)
- Reconstructing start/end indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const from = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  const bestIdx = new Array(k+1).fill(-1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      from[i][t] = {type:'skip', at:i-1, t};
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++){
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - pref[i-1];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) { bestVal[t] = val; bestIdx[t] = i-1; }
      const cand = bestVal[t] + pref[i] - c;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = cand;
        from[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i-1, start:bestIdx[t], t};
      }
    }
  }
  let i = n, t = k;
  const blocks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t > 0){
    const info = from[i][t];
    if (!info) break;
    if (info.type === 'end'){
      blocks.push([info.start, info.end]);
      i = info.start;
      t--;
    } else {
      i = info.at;
    }
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for at-most-k blocks?
- How would you handle negative values of c or allow zero-cost blocks?
- What modifications optimize space to O(k) while preserving reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming over non-overlapping subarrays where block value depends on internal structure, requiring precomputation and reconstruction techniques.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping k blocks in increasing order
- Precomputation of per-block value (peaks inside)
- DP with inner maximization over l
- Reconstruction pointers; time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueSubarrays(A, k, F, P) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const peaks = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Precompute peaks
  for (let i = 1; i < n-1; i++) {
    if (A[i-1] < A[i] && A[i] > A[i+1]) {
      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        for (let r = i; r < n; r++) {
          peaks[l][r]++;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const val = (l, r) => pref[r+1] - pref[l] - F - P * peaks[l][r];

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1) > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1);
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct
  const blocks = [];
  let cur = n, curT = k;
  while (curT > 0 && parent[cur][curT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[cur][curT];
    blocks.unshift([start, cur-1]);
    cur = start;
    curT--;
  }

  return { max: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Complexity

- Time: O(n²k) due to nested loops for peaks, DP, and reconstruction
- Space: O(nk) for DP table and parent pointers, reducible to O(n) with rolling arrays

The key challenge is efficiently computing block values while maintaining optimal substructure across k non-overlapping segments.

## Why This Is Asked
